  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      These two are closer competitors than most pairs on this site: both sell data by the gigabyte rather than unlimited-first. <strong>Saily</strong> is usually a bit cheaper per GB and bundles ad/malware blocking into every plan, since it's built by the same company as NordVPN. <strong>Airalo</strong> has the bigger, more established country-by-country eSIM catalog and its own quirky referral-code ecosystem. If you'd rather never think about a data meter at all, neither is the right pick: see <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a> instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A VPN Company vs. an eSIM Specialist</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo was built specifically to sell travel eSIMs and has been at it longer, with a deep catalog of individual country and regional profiles. Saily is Nord Security's eSIM product, the same company behind NordVPN, and every Saily plan ships with ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool built in rather than sold separately. If you'd normally pay for a VPN app when traveling anyway, that's already in the box with Saily. If you just want the cheapest possible GB and don't care about the extras, it's not a reason to pick one over the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saily also sells one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, rather than requiring a fresh profile per region. Airalo's model leans more on picking the right local, regional, or global eSIM upfront for your specific trip.
  </p>

